      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s first American settlers arrived in the 1820s and 1830s, drawn by vast timber stands and new farming opportunities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/wallace_florida",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wallace sits just east of the Escambia River, a waterway that shapes local land use and flood patterns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/wallace_florida",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The region’s humid subtropical climate drives local environmental management plans aimed at resilience against flooding and erosion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/wallace_florida",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Escambia River borders Wallace’s western edge, historically serving as a transportation route for early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
